WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.780
Near the end of a job interview, the panel asks: do you have any questions for us?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.520
The candidate asks something,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.290
or says: No, I think you’ve covered everything.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.450
A doctoral thesis from the School of Continuous Improvement asks what that question does,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.660
and whether it ever changes who gets the job.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.980
For nine years, I scribed for selection panels.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.860
The form had no box for the candidate’s questions,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.450
and I wrote none of them down.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.600
She recorded four hundred and twelve interviews at twenty-six employers.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.454
Nearly nine in ten questions were of six kinds,

00:00:33.654 --> 00:00:36.164
led by: what are the next steps?

00:00:36.914 --> 00:00:40.404
Before the invitation, panellists sealed a score in an envelope,

00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:42.904
then scored again once the candidate had left.

00:00:43.654 --> 00:00:47.384
Ninety-five point seven per cent of the scores did not move.

00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:51.624
In fifty-two recruitment rounds, the question never changed who came first.

00:00:52.374 --> 00:00:55.392
What the question does is carried by the panel’s answer.

00:00:55.692 --> 00:00:58.822
Ninety point four per cent of offers were accepted after a specific answer.

00:00:59.022 --> 00:01:01.452
Seventy-three point five, without one.

00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:03.822
Then a trial scored the question.

00:01:04.122 --> 00:01:05.462
Every candidate asked one,

00:01:05.662 --> 00:01:07.622
and fifty-eight asked the same thing:

00:01:07.822 --> 00:01:10.802
what does success look like in this role in the first ninety days?

00:01:12.102 --> 00:01:14.972
The University has now adopted the Candidate Question Standard:

00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:17.812
two questions from a Schedule of twenty-four,

00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:19.212
answered from a Register,

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.982
and rated for twenty per cent of the selection total.

00:01:22.732 --> 00:01:28.662
Every selection interview the University conducts now closes in these words, and no others:

00:01:28.962 --> 00:01:31.382
You may now ask the panel your two questions.
